#### Grammar 1: -는다고/ㄴ다고/다고 하다
**Meaning and Usage:** This is used with an action or descriptive verb and is used to indirectly relay information one has heard from another person. It is also used when a subject shows his or her thought or opinion about what he or she has heard as an indirect expression in a declarative sentence.

**Form:** '-ㄴ다고 하다' is used when the action verb stem ends with 'ㄹ' or a vowel and '-는다고 하다' is used when it ends with a consonant. '-다고 하다' is used after a descriptive verb stem.

**Examples:**
1. 에릭이 선생님께 날마다 한국어 신문을 읽는다고 합니다. - Eric told his teacher that he reads the Korean newspaper every day.
2. 유키가 한국과 일본의 날씨는 비슷하다고 해요. - Yuki says that the weather in Korea is similar to that of Japan.
3. 존이 먹고 싶은 음식은 중국 음식이라고 해. - John says that the food he wants to eat is Chinese food.
4. 어제 어머니께서 저에게 여름 옷을 부쳤다고 하셨어요. - My mother told me she sent me my summer clothes yesterday.
5. 가: 리타오도 오늘 저녁 모임에 와요? / 나: 네, 리타오한테서 연락이 왔는데 리타오도 오늘 모임에 온다고 해요. - A: Is Letao coming to the meeting tonight? / B: Yes, I got a call from him and he said that he was coming tonight.
6. 가: 노라 씨가 등산을 갈까? / 나: 노라 씨는 등산을 가지 않는다고 해요. - A: Will Nora go hiking? / B: Nora said that she would not go hiking.

**Note:** The negative form for action verbs is '-지 않는다고 하다' and the negative form for descriptive verbs is '–지 않다고 하다.' If it is combined with a noun, you use 'N이라고/라고 하다' and the negative form is 'N이/가 아니라고 하다.'

#### Grammar 2: -냐고 하다
**Meaning and Usage:** This is used with an action or descriptive verb as an indirect expression to indirectly relay what one has heard as a question from another person.

**Examples:**
1. 동생이 저에게 몇 시까지 수업을 듣냐고 해요. - My younger sister asked me what time my class was over.
2. 아버지께서 저에게 요즘 많이 바쁘냐고 하셨어요. - My father asked me if I was very busy these days.
3. 사무실 직원이 존 씨에게 전화번호가 몇 번이냐고 했어요. - The office worker asked John what his telephone number was.
4. 한국에 오기 전에 무슨 일을 했냐고 저에게 물어보는 사람들이 많아요. - There are a lot of people who ask me what kind of work I did before I came to Korea.
5. 가: 에릭한테 주말에 소풍을 갈 수 있냐고 물어봤어? / 나: 응, 에릭도 주말에 소풍을 갈 수 있다고 했어. - A: Did you ask Eric if he was able to go on a picnic over the weekend? / B: Yes, Eric said he could go on a picnic over the weekend.
6. 가: 유키 씨가 뭐라고 했어요? / 나: 유키 씨가 저에게 불고기를 어떻게 만드냐고 했어요. - A: What did Yuki say? / B: Yuki asked me how to make bulgogi.

**Note:** '-느냐고 하다' is used with action verbs including '있다' and '없다.' '-으냐고 하다' can be used when the descriptive verb stem ends with consonants except for 'ㄹ.'

### <2> She is suggesting that all of us go on a picnic

#### Dialogue
**Eric:** From whom did you just receive a text message?

**Yuki:** Nora sent me the text message. She is suggesting that all our classmates go on a picnic this Saturday.

**Eric:** That's a good idea. I heard that there were many people going for an outing to the Han River Park or Namsan because the weather is nice these days.

**Yuki:** Then should I suggest to everyone that we go to the Han River camping site?

**Eric:** Yes, it will be fun to ride bicycles and have a barbeque party there.

**Yuki:** Okay, then I'll contact everyone and tell them to bring anyone they want to come with.

