#### Grammar 1: 접속사
**Meaning and Usage:** This is a word that connects two sentences. Examples of conjunctions are '그래도, 그래서, 그러나, 그러니까, 그런데, 그렇지만, and 그리고.'

- **그래도:** This is used when the content of the second sentence is unrelated to the first one.
- **그래서:** This is used when the content in the first sentence is the cause or reason for the second one.
- **그러나:** This is used when the two sentences are opposites.
- **그러니까:** This is used when the first sentence is the reason or reference for the second one.
- **그런데:** This is used when the content of the second sentence is different from the first one.
- **그렇지만:** This is used when acknowledging the content of the first sentence while making an opposing statement in the second one.
- **그리고:** This is used when an additional information is added after the first sentence.
- **왜냐하면:** This is used when the second sentence is the reason or reference for the first one.

**Examples:**
1. 열이 많이 납니다. 그리고 기침도 심합니다. - I have a high fever. And my cough is also severe.
2. 다음 주에 동창회를 할 거예요. 그러니까 꼭 오세요. - We are having an alumnus meeting next week so please come.
3. 비빔냉면을 다 못 먹었다. 왜냐하면 너무 매웠기 때문이다. - I couldn't finish eating the bibim-naengmyeon because it was too spicy.
4. 친구와 콘서트를 보러 갔다. 그러나 남은 표가 없어서 그냥 집으로 돌아와야 했다. - I went to see a concert with my friend. But because there were no more tickets left, we had to just come back home.
5. 가: 여행을 가고 싶지요? / 나: 네, 그렇지만 시간이 없어요. - A: You want to go on a trip, don't you? / B: Yes, but I don't have time.
6. 가: 너무 피곤해서 그냥 자고 싶어요. / 나: 그래도 씻고 주무세요. - A: I am so tired that I just want to go to sleep. / B: You should still wash up and then go to sleep.

#### Grammar 2: -을/ㄹ 테니까
**Meaning and Usage:** This is used with an action or descriptive verb when connecting an uncompleted work as the reason for the following sentence. When the subject in the first sentence is in the first-person, it shows the will or plan of the speaker and when it is in the third-person, it shows the conjecture of the speaker.

**Form:** '-ㄹ 테니까' is used when the action or descriptive verb stem ends with a vowel or 'ㄹ' and '-을 테니까' is used when it ends with a consonant.

**Examples:**
1. 호텔은 제가 예약할 테니까 유키 씨는 렌터카를 예약해 주세요. - I'll make the reservations for the hotel, so Yuki, please make the reservations for the rental car.
2. 제가 김밥을 만들어 올 테니까 흐엉 씨는 음료수를 준비해 주세요. - I'll make the gimbap and come, so Huang, please prepare the drinks.
3. 주말에는 극장에 사람이 많을 테니까 평일에 가세요. - There will be many people at the theater on the weekends so go on the weekday.
4. 지금 가면 차가 밀릴 테니까 지하철로 갑시다. - If we go now there will be traffic, so let's take the subway.
5. 가: 잠깐만 기다려 주시겠어요? / 나: 네, 커피를 마시면서 기다릴 테니까 천천히 오세요. - A: Can you wait for a little while? / B: Yes. I'll be waiting while I drink coffee so take your time.
6. 가: 지금 밤 11시인데 노라 씨에게 전화해도 될까요? / 나: 지금은 노라 씨가 자고 있을 테니까 내일 전화하세요. - A: It's 11 o'clock at night right now but is it okay to call Nora? / B: Nora will be sleeping so call her tomorrow.

**Note:** When the first sentence is in the first-person, it is not combined with a descriptive verb. It can be combined when the subject is in the third-person. Ex) 제가 이따가 배가 고플 테니까 김밥을 사 가지고 가겠습니다. (x) 날씨가 따뜻할 테니까 너무 두꺼운 옷은 가지고 가지 마세요. (o)